          <xpath>Defs/PawnKindDef[defName="AA_Behemoth"]/lifeStages/li[1]/bodyGraphicData/drawSize</xpath>
          <value><drawSize>10.81</drawSize></value>
        </li>
        <li Class="PatchOperationReplace">
          <xpath>Defs/PawnKindDef[defName="AA_Behemoth"]/lifeStages/li[2]/bodyGraphicData/drawSize</xpath>
          <value><drawSize>14.90</drawSize></value>
        </li>
        <li Class="PatchOperationReplace">
          <xpath>Defs/PawnKindDef[defName="AA_Behemoth"]/lifeStages/li[3]/bodyGraphicData/drawSize</xpath>
          <value><drawSize>18.06</drawSize></value>
        </li>
        <!-- 🔴 bodySize AND healthScale, "fully", on his instruction. This file's standing rule
             is that drawSize and bodySize are NEVER merged - drawSize is the picture, bodySize
             is meat, leather, hunting yield, carrying capacity, food need and melee scaling.
             ⭐ That rule is not being broken, it is being INVOKED: he asked for the mechanical
             half explicitly and by name, which is exactly the case the rule reserves.

             Matched numerically to adult drawSize, which is this file's own precedent - Zakkeg
             carries drawSize 8.20 and baseBodySize 8.2. Alpha Animals shipped 8 / 10; both
             double, to 16 / 20.

             ⚠️ WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: melee damage. RimWorld tool `power` is a flat per-tool
             number and does NOT scale with bodySize, so "heaviest hitter" here means it is
             enormously durable and enormously heavy, not that it swings harder. Raising the
             dragonclaw tools is a separate, deliberate combat change - ask before doing it. -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
          <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="AA_Behemoth"]/race/baseBodySize</xpath>
          <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
            <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="AA_Behemoth"]/race/baseBodySize</xpath>
            <value><baseBodySize>32</baseBodySize></value>
